export const body = "<p>Chapter 02: Level Design Basics</p>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n\n<p>Before art assets can be integrated, the design of the level must be finalized. The goal of this chapter is to provide you enough vocabulary to communicate with level designers and make suggestions as you see fit.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ee787d006c115af8b5b1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ee787d006c115af8b5b1.webp\" width=\"868\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:519px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Blockout</h2>\n\n<p>A level blockout is a rough 3D layout built with primitive blocks. By using simple geometry, level designers can iterate their layouts more efficiently.</p>\n\n<p>Although also called “whitebox” or “graybox,” blockouts still have colors to help viewers distinguish different elements. In the opposite blockout, green is <strong>grass</strong>, red is <strong>enemy</strong>, and yellow is <strong>climbable objects</strong>.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/548ef0b0764e6c203e3a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/548ef0b0764e6c203e3a.webp\" width=\"902\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:538px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – Blockouts</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b1455ee7f20eeed52375.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b1455ee7f20eeed52375.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"435\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<h2>Beat doc</h2>\n\n<p>A beat is an event that takes place in the level (a cinematic, an encounter, a puzzle). A beat doc is a rundown of all the beats in a level.</p>\n\n<p>In these two examples, we can see the levels are divided into smaller areas, each dedicated to a specific gameplay type (exploration, puzzle, or combat).</p>\n\n<p>With a beat doc, the level designer can anticipate how fast or slow his level is going to play out, and how varied the gameplay is going to be.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ae2ac7e206a315deaab6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ae2ac7e206a315deaab6.webp\" width=\"1007\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – Beat doc</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7ba44fc2a71a2a87c9bd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7ba44fc2a71a2a87c9bd.webp\" width=\"840\" height=\"476\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/dfe0d86c918c7ad758a7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/dfe0d86c918c7ad758a7.webp\" width=\"852\" height=\"476\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<h2>Metrics</h2>\n\n<p>Metrics are the measurements that define game world, including all geometry and characters. How high Mario can jump, or how low Nathan Drake can duck, for example. And how tall or short the player character is, or how large or small a building is.</p>\n\n<p>Metrics not only drive the gameplay but also make a game world look and feel believable, regardless of the chosen art style.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Must-know metrics</strong>:</p>\n\n<ul><li>Character height</li><li>Average wall height, width, depth and length</li><li>Door and doorway height and width</li><li>Stair step height and depth</li></ul>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3c41a64b9c482e110112.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3c41a64b9c482e110112.webp\" width=\"790\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:472px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – Beat doc</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3be3881548876e7ecfa7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3be3881548876e7ecfa7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"449\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<h2>Scale</h2>\n\n<p>Scale refers to the size of an object relative to another object. For example, the size of the player character vs. the size of a doorway. Make sure that accessible doors are tall and wide enough for your character to walk through.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/961acb79e8835611d263.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/961acb79e8835611d263.webp\" width=\"840\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:499px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Collision detection</h2>\n\n<h3>What is it?</h3>\n\n<p>Collision detection is a system that <strong>calculates interactions between different objects</strong> in a game. That said, not every in-game mesh requires collision. Minor meshes (that players aren’t going to play with) can skip collision to help reach the performance target.</p>\n\n<p>You can think of it like this: a 3D model without collision is like a hologram; you can see it, but you can’t touch it. With collision activated, that model gains interactive properties (which are defined by programmers).</p>\n\n<p>Collison properties define the properties of a surface, including but not limited to physics, sound and VFX.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e1b9fb0a241ecaee114a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e1b9fb0a241ecaee114a.webp\" width=\"626\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:374px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>How does it work?</h3>\n\n<p>There are 2 key things to define for collision detection:</p>\n\n<ul><li>The boundary of <strong>where collision can occur </strong>to the object?</li><li><strong>What event should be triggered</strong> when different boundaries intersect with each other?</li></ul>\n\n<p>For example, what should happen when your character run into a wall? Some games will let the character keep running so long as the player pushes forward. In other games, the characters may stop moving altogether, and there can be a dedicated animation for that like<em>…</em></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/84b70129dcd9cac910ff.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/84b70129dcd9cac910ff.webp\" width=\"526\" height=\"295\" style=\"--source-width:526px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ce1fb3a6470ba8d2e7c6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ce1fb3a6470ba8d2e7c6.webp\" width=\"450\" height=\"253\" style=\"--source-width:450px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>…like the character flattening himself against the wall.</p>\n\n<h3>How to use it?</h3>\n\n<p>Game engines often provide a number of collision geometry for both simple and complex meshes. For example, Unreal Engine offers 3 basic shapes—sphere, capsule, and box—for static and simple-shaped meshes. As for complex meshes, such as a chair, there has to be manual configuration to tune the collision geometry precisely.</p>\n\n<p>In Unity, the term for collision geometry is “collider.”</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5904f1f2dbf7e5bc8d44.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5904f1f2dbf7e5bc8d44.webp\" width=\"978\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Camera systems</h2>\n\n<h3>Types</h3>\n\n<p>To keep things simple, we will divide game cameras into 2 categories:</p>\n\n<p>1.<strong>Variable</strong>: the player can control the camera (first-person or third-person)</p>\n\n<p>2.<strong>Non-variable</strong>: the player cannot control the camera</p>\n\n<p>Even within the same category, camera systems vary from game to game. For example, the camera in <em>The Last of Us II</em> stays close to the player character while the camera in <em>Control</em> tends pull away to reveal more of the environment. Both are third-person but not the same.</p>\n\n<p>Since the camera system can impact how your levels and art asset are made, you should study it thoroughly in advance.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f1ce3b66749d4d90fc5e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f1ce3b66749d4d90fc5e.webp\" width=\"884\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:528px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXTRA</strong><strong> – </strong><strong>Sometimes both</strong></p>\n\n<p>Although most games only use one type of camera, some games have both. In this case, you will need to understand the specific behaviors of both types.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7fa3bbf3c2a0017c5242.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7fa3bbf3c2a0017c5242.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Closer means more detailed</h3>\n\n<p>Although there are many different camera systems, what level artists should focus on remains unchanged: the level of detail relative to camera distance. Basically, the closer players can get to an object, the more detailed it must be.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c861bd6420e9970a61fe.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c861bd6420e9970a61fe.webp\" width=\"912\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:545px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Players rarely look up</h3>\n\n<p>Unless there is something (like an enemy or a UI element) beckoning at the player, they will often keep the camera at eye level. Therefore, when you place key items in your environments, you should consider whether it is visible within the default camera view.</p>\n\n<p>Here is an example from <em>Resident Evil Village </em>where the player has to look for a key that was placed slightly higher than eye level. That said, this room is occupied by a 3 meters tall vampire lady, so it makes sense that her key is hung slightly higher.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a3ccc1573e0be500693e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a3ccc1573e0be500693e.webp\" width=\"886\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:530px\" alt=\"Illustration 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXTRA</strong><strong> – </strong><strong>Linearity</strong></p>\n\n<p>Linearity refers to how straightforward the progression of a game is. If a level simply demands players to go straight from A to B, then it’s linear. In contrast, if a level doesn’t put its players on a set path, allowing them to progress at their will, then it’s non-linear.</p>\n\n<p>But it’s important to note that there is no clear-cut categorization for linearity. So it’s more advantageous to view it as a spectrum.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/772056f1fac56990dffb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/772056f1fac56990dffb.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"219\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 18\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><em>The Last of Us 2</em> also divides its story into parts called “chapters.” While each chapter has a definitive path of progression, the design is more open than that of <em>DMC5</em>. There are several reasons:</p>\n\n<ul><li><em>TLOU2</em> is a stealth/action game, so the levels need more square meters to facilitate that playstyle.</li><li>The game promotes exploration for refilling ammo and finding crafting materials.</li></ul>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c898ba1e343b3b237ab5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c898ba1e343b3b237ab5.webp\" width=\"873\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:493px\" alt=\"Illustration 19\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><em>Dark Souls </em>takes openness on step further, designing its world map with interconnected branching paths and shortcuts.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/90b0e76bb1f028d713d2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/90b0e76bb1f028d713d2.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"337\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 20\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><em>The Outer Worlds</em> adopts the typical open-world design where there is a main quest line and a wide range of side quests. The player is allowed to go back and forth between the main storyline and the optional content however they desire.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/44feee8d67d033840aad.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/44feee8d67d033840aad.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 21\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><em>The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild</em> is one of the most non-linear games ever made. After the tutorial, you are free to roam the land of Hyrule. You only have one objective that is beating the main villain Ganon. There are quests scattered throughout the world, but they are all optional, meaning you can skip them all and get straight to Ganon if you so wish.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/27ff77138102e95a40ce.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/27ff77138102e95a40ce.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 22\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>";
export const images = ["assets/library-inline/27ff77138102e95a40ce.webp", "assets/library-inline/3be3881548876e7ecfa7.webp", "assets/library-inline/3c41a64b9c482e110112.webp", "assets/library-inline/44feee8d67d033840aad.webp", "assets/library-inline/548ef0b0764e6c203e3a.webp", "assets/library-inline/5904f1f2dbf7e5bc8d44.webp", "assets/library-inline/772056f1fac56990dffb.webp", "assets/library-inline/7ba44fc2a71a2a87c9bd.webp", "assets/library-inline/7fa3bbf3c2a0017c5242.webp", "assets/library-inline/84b70129dcd9cac910ff.webp", "assets/library-inline/90b0e76bb1f028d713d2.webp", "assets/library-inline/961acb79e8835611d263.webp", "assets/library-inline/a3ccc1573e0be500693e.webp", "assets/library-inline/ae2ac7e206a315deaab6.webp", "assets/library-inline/b1455ee7f20eeed52375.webp", "assets/library-inline/c861bd6420e9970a61fe.webp", "assets/library-inline/c898ba1e343b3b237ab5.webp", "assets/library-inline/ce1fb3a6470ba8d2e7c6.webp", "assets/library-inline/dfe0d86c918c7ad758a7.webp", "assets/library-inline/e1b9fb0a241ecaee114a.webp", "assets/library-inline/ee787d006c115af8b5b1.webp", "assets/library-inline/f1ce3b66749d4d90fc5e.webp"];
